<h2>Risk, Strategy and Portfolio Thinking</h2>

<section class="lesson-card">
    <h3>Understanding Investment Risk</h3>

    <p>
        All investing involves risk. Risk refers to the possibility that an investment may lose
        value or perform worse than expected. When investors buy stocks, funds, or other assets,
        they accept uncertainty about future prices.
    </p>

    <p>
        In general, investments with higher potential returns also come with higher risk.
        For example, individual stocks may grow quickly but can also drop significantly in value.
        More diversified investments, such as funds, tend to reduce the impact of a single
        company performing poorly.
    </p>

    <h4>Short‑Term vs Long‑Term Risk</h4>

    <p>
        In the short term, markets can be very volatile. Prices may move up or down due to
        news, economic changes, or investor sentiment. However, historically the stock
        market has tended to grow over longer time horizons.
    </p>

    <p>
        Because of this, many investors focus on long‑term investing rather than trying to
        predict short‑term price movements.
    </p>

    <h4>Risk Tolerance</h4>

    <p>
        Risk tolerance describes how comfortable an investor is with market fluctuations.
        Some investors prefer stable investments with smaller returns, while others are
        comfortable with larger swings in value if it means the possibility of higher growth.
    </p>
</section>

<h2>Investment Strategy and Portfolio Thinking</h2>
<section class="lesson-card">
    <h3> Portfolio </h3>
    <p>
        Instead of focusing on a single investment, many investors think in terms of a
        portfolio. A portfolio is the collection of all investments an individual owns,
        such as stocks, funds, or bonds.
    </p>

    <p>
        A key idea in portfolio management is diversification. Diversification means
        spreading investments across different companies, sectors, or asset types to
        reduce overall risk.
    </p>

    <h4>Diversification</h4>

    <p>
        If an investor places all of their money into one company, the success of their
        investment depends entirely on that company. However, by investing in multiple
        companies or funds, the impact of one poor investment becomes smaller.
    </p>

    <h4>Long‑Term Strategy</h4>

    <p>
        Many successful investors follow a consistent long‑term strategy. This can include
        investing regularly, maintaining diversification, and avoiding emotional decisions
        during market volatility.
    </p>
</section>